  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Surface cold front has made some southward \nprogression this afternoon and now sits just north of Highway 24\nthis hour per mesoanalysis and surface observations. Cooling \nstratus, as well as outflow and torrential rain from \nthunderstorms progressing northward, has cooled much of the area\ndown into the upper 70s to low 80s but a sliver of upper 80s \nwith Tds hovering near 80 is still hanging on south of this \nboundary. Opted to cancel the Heat Advisory for all but these\ncoastal counties through 8PM, although these may also be dropped\nahead of schedule as convection fills back in.\n\nBeyond today, oppressive heat indices above 100 appear unlikely\n(less than 20%) through the next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Forecast through tomorrow will be heavily\ndependent on various mesoscale features, which in turn will\ndictate where stronger storms and flooding will be focused.\nFluctuation's in risk areas are likely as ongoing convective\nevolution is analyzed.\n\nEnvironment today has indeed been favorable for strong storms\nand torrential rainfall. Convection got an early start,\ndeveloping as early as 11 AM and blossoming along the stalled\nfrontal boundary, then compounding with convergent outflows and\ndiurnal sound and sea breezes. Overall environment remains very\nfavorable for a severe and flooding risk to continue with PWATs\nof 2-2.25\" observed, hefty MLCAPEs of 2500-3000 J/kg, and\nrespectable deep layer shear for the summer (25-35 kt). The\nprimary threat remains damaging winds primarily due to wet\nmicrobursts, but some cells today have been rotation and could\npose a hail risk. Additionally, with so many boundaries in play\norientated in various directions cannot rule out a threat for a\nbrief, short-lived tornado or two.\n\nThe combination of strong instability and an anomalously moist \nairmass should continue to support the potential of 2-3\"/hr \nrainfall rates. While thunderstorms are likely to propagate \nalong various outflows and mesoscale boundaries, those rates \nshould support a risk of flooding of urban and poor drainage \nareas, with an associated risk for flash flooding. This risk \nlooks to be the highest in some of the more urbanized areas from\nJacksonville to New Bern and north through Kinston and \nGreenville. Current QPE trends suggest the Flood Watch remains\nin good shape today, and no changes to headlines were made.\n\nEvolution beyond this afternoon's activity remains a question\nmark, although there is a slightly stronger signal for\nconvection, growing upscale across across upstate SC, to\nprogress along the stalled frontal boundary and impact\nsoutheastern NC after nightfall. Instability will remain\nelevated tonight but there will be a modest decrease in shear.\nStill, a marginal threat for damaging winds will remain with\nthis complex. Additionally, it will not take much additional\nrainfall to aggravate flooding concerns for those locales who\nalready saw appreciable amounts this afternoon.\n\nConvective threat remains tomorrow as front remains stalled over\nthe region. Like today, convection could get an early start as\nsoon as 15z and spread inland through the afternoon. Shear will\nbe quite a bit weaker and the severe threat will be less than\n5%, but high forecast PWATS point to another day of efficient\nrainmakers and potential flooding concerns.",
